				"⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				355,
				2
			],
			"Message": "feat(Common): Add W3C traceparent builder for cross-tier distributed tracing\n\nExtend the `Telemetry` module in Common with a new `Traceparent` component to standardize distributed context propagation across tier boundaries (Mountain ↔ Cocoon, Mountain ↔ Sky, Sidecars). Implement `Build` and `Parse` functions for W3C version-00 `traceparent` headers, ensuring every process maintains a stable `TraceId` while minting unique `SpanId` values for each inter-process call.\n\nThis addition unifies the observability surface by allowing OTLP and PostHog events to share a consistent trace identity, enabling end-to-end tracing from the editor UI through the native backend and into the extension host. The implementation derives the trace ID deterministically from the process ID and current nanosecond timestamp to ensure agreement between the OTLP exporter and the header builder, even across separate modules.",
			"SHA": "490bcfbfeb03025ac7e8a7c0c4127b1bc32bd077"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-05-03T07:02:32Z",
			"File": [
				"⠃⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				354,
				17
			],
			"Message": "feat(Common): Introduce shared dual-pipe telemetry module for sidecars\n\nAdd a new `Telemetry` module to the Common crate to standardize observability across all Rust sidecars (Air, Echo, Rest, Grove, Mist) and the Mountain backend. This module implements a dual-pipe architecture supporting both PostHog for product analytics and OTLP for distributed tracing.\n\nThe implementation provides a runtime-configured client initialized once per process via `Initialize::Fn`, which stamps every event with the specific `Tier` (e.g., 'air', 'echo') and standard Land identity properties. It includes helper functions for capturing events (`CaptureEvent`), errors (`CaptureError`), and session starts (`CaptureSession`), alongside a fire-and-forget OTLP span exporter (`EmitOTLPSpan`).\n\nKey architectural decisions include:\n- `cfg!(debug_assertions)` gating to completely strip telemetry logic from release builds.\n- Runtime configuration via environment variables for PostHog and OTLP endpoints.\n- Atomic caching of configuration and a `OnceLock` singleton for the PostHog client to minimize boot overhead.\n- Stable distinct ID generation to unify user sessions across multiple sidecar processes.\n\nThis change unifies the telemetry surface, replacing ad-hoc logging with a robust, dashboard-ready pipeline for the Errors & Reliability and Boot & Startup Performance dashboards.",
			"SHA": "f058bde8ec77ec51c661c57d4f193b5b777a610b"
		},
